| **SESSION 3: AI for Innovative Learning Design** |
| --- |

| **Asynchronous Resource  │  Available: w/c 17 March 2026  │  Due: Monday 30 March 2026, 9:00am** |
| --- |

## **Session Overview**

This is an asynchronous session. Your completed response to this resource serves as your attendance and participation record for Session 3. You must submit by the deadline above.

| **Learning Objectives** |
| --- |
| Understand how AI can be used to generate diverse, high-quality learning resources quickly. Apply AI tools to design at least one innovative, curriculum-aligned learning activity. Critically evaluate the ethical considerations of AI in curriculum design. Maintain ethical standards and transparency in your AI-assisted practice. |

## **What You Need to Do**

This session has three stages. Work through them in order over the next two weeks.

| **STAGE 1  —  Watch ****&**** Explore** | Watch the CPD resources below (approx. 45–60 mins total). You do not need to watch every video in full — use the descriptions to prioritise based on your current confidence level. |
| --- | --- |
| **STAGE 2  —  Create** | Use an AI tool to design one learning activity or resource for a real class. This could be a differentiated task, an AI-generated quiz, a challenging scenario, or a discussion prompt. Screenshot or save your output. |
| **STAGE 3  —  Reflect ****&**** Submit** | Complete the submission form (link at the end of this document) with your evidence and reflection. This is your attendance record for Session 3. |

## **CPD Resources**

Work through the resources below. They are grouped by theme. You are not expected to complete every resource — choose at least one from each theme.

| **📌  A note on YouTube resources** |
| --- |
| YouTube links in this document open a pre-populated search rather than a fixed video URL. This is intentional — it ensures you always reach current, active content rather than a video that may have been removed. When you follow a YouTube search link: use YouTube’s date filter (‘This year’ or ‘Last year’) and choose a video with strong engagement (views, likes, recent comments). The description for each resource gives you clear guidance on what to look for. |

### **Theme A: Using AI to Generate Learning Resources**

These resources focus on the practical mechanics of using AI to design and create curriculum materials.

| **#** | **Resource** |
| --- | --- |
| **1** | [**Search YouTube: “AI lesson planning for teachers ChatGPT Copilot 2024”**](https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AI+lesson+planning+for+teachers+ChatGPT+Copilot+2024)  (YouTube search — select a recent video (~10–20 min)) — Search this term on YouTube and choose a recent, well-rated tutorial (posted 2024 or later). Look for walkthroughs covering prompt writing, differentiation, and rubric generation. Suggested channels: Eduaide, Alice Keeler, or Ditch That Textbook. |
| **2** | [**Search YouTube: “AI tools for teachers creating learning resources 2024”**](https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AI+tools+for+teachers+creating+learning+resources+2024)  (YouTube search — select a recent video (~15–20 min)) — Search this term and select a video demonstrating practical AI resource creation (scenario tasks, formative checks, visual explainers). Filter by ‘This year’ or ‘Last year’ in YouTube’s date filter to ensure relevance. |
| **3** | [**Introduction to Prompt Engineering for Generative AI — LinkedIn Learning (Ronnie Sheer)**](https://www.linkedin.com/learning/introduction-to-prompt-engineering-for-generative-ai)  (LinkedIn Learning — ~1 hr 7 min (dip in)) — The most-taken AI course on LinkedIn Learning 2025. Focus on the first three modules if time is limited: these cover constructing effective prompts and iterating outputs — directly applicable to resource generation. |

### **Theme B: AI, Curriculum Design ****&**** Critical Thinking**

These resources explore how AI can be used not just to generate content, but to design richer, more challenging learning experiences that promote critical thinking.

| **#** | **Resource** |
| --- | --- |
| **4** | [**Search YouTube: “ISTE AI innovative learning design Bloom’s taxonomy educators”**](https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ISTE+AI+innovative+learning+design+Bloom%27s+taxonomy+educators)  (YouTube search — select a recent video (~15–25 min)) — Search this term and select an ISTE or similar conference session on AI-powered learning design. Look for videos that connect AI tool use to cognitive challenge, Bloom’s Taxonomy, or Universal Design for Learning (UDL). |
| **5** | [**Generative AI for Business Leaders — LinkedIn Learning (Tomer Cohen, LinkedIn CEO)**](https://www.linkedin.com/learning/generative-ai-for-business-leaders)  (LinkedIn Learning — ~35 min) — Highly accessible overview of how generative AI transforms design and decision-making. Adapt the thinking here to curriculum design: how do you move from ‘using AI’ to ‘designing with AI’? |
| **6** | [**Search YouTube: “AI curriculum design activity builder Eduaide teachers 2024”**](https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AI+curriculum+design+activity+builder+Eduaide+teachers+2024)  (YouTube search — select a recent video (~10–15 min)) — Search this term and select a video demonstrating an education-specific AI platform (e.g. Eduaide.ai, Curipod, or MagicSchool) being used to build structured, progressive learning activities. Focus on videos that show iterative prompt refinement. |

### **Theme C: Ethical AI Use in Education**

These resources address the ethical responsibilities educators have when using AI, including transparency, bias, academic integrity, and maintaining your professional judgement.

| **#** | **Resource** |
| --- | --- |
| **7** | [**Ethics in the Age of Generative AI — LinkedIn Learning (Vilas Dhar)**](https://www.linkedin.com/learning/ethics-in-the-age-of-generative-ai)  (LinkedIn Learning — ~40 min) — One of LinkedIn Learning’s top 10 most-taken AI courses globally in 2025. Provides a three-part framework for analysing ethical AI decisions — directly applicable to decisions you make as an educator about AI use. |
| **8** | [**Search YouTube: “AI bias education teachers classroom 2024”**](https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AI+bias+education+teachers+classroom+2024)  (YouTube search — select a recent video (~10–20 min)) — Search this term and select a clear, accessible video explaining how bias enters AI-generated content and why this matters when designing learning materials. Filter by date to prioritise 2024–2025 uploads for the most current perspective. |
| **9** | [**Teaching AI Ethics — Leon Furze (Article Series, updated 2025)**](https://leonfurze.com/ai-ethics/)  (Web — Read 1–2 articles (~20 min)) — Highly regarded, regularly updated resource by UK-based educator and AI specialist Leon Furze. Each article is short and practical. Recommended starting points: ‘Bias’ and ‘Truth and GenAI’. Excellent for connecting ethical considerations directly to classroom practice. |

## **Your Task: Design with AI**

After engaging with the CPD resources, complete the following task. You will submit evidence of this in the submission form.

| **Task Instructions** |
| --- |
| **Choose a real class or unit you are currently teaching or will teach soon.** Use an AI tool (e.g. Microsoft Copilot, ChatGPT, Claude, Eduaide.ai) to design ONE of the following: A differentiated task or activity for mixed-ability learners A challenging scenario or problem-based task that promotes critical thinking A formative assessment activity (e.g. quiz, discussion prompt, self-assessment) A set of tiered extension questions for an existing assessment brief **Save a screenshot or copy of the AI-generated output AND your final edited version (showing your professional input).** Briefly annotate or note: what prompt you used, what you changed, and why. |

## **Assessment ****&**** Evidence of Understanding**

Your submission form will ask you to respond to the following four questions. These are your evidence of understanding and replace a quiz or test for this session. Take approximately 10–15 minutes per response — quality over length.

| **Reflection Questions (answer all four in the submission form)** |
| --- |
| 1.  Which AI tool(s) did you use and why? What did you notice about the quality and limitations of the output? 2.  How did you adapt or improve the AI-generated content to better meet your learners’ needs? What pedagogical decisions did you make? 3.  What ethical considerations arose as you used AI for this task (e.g. accuracy, bias, transparency with students, academic integrity)? How did you or would you address them? 4.  How will this experience influence how you use AI in your curriculum planning going forward? Identify one concrete next step. |

## **Marking Rubric**

Your submission will be assessed against the following criteria. A Pass-equivalent response demonstrates completion with basic engagement. Aim for Merit or Distinction.

| **Criteria** | **Distinction** | **Merit** | **Pass** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **AI Tool Application** | Selects and applies at least two AI tools with clear pedagogical justification, demonstrating critical evaluation of each tool's strengths and limitations. | Selects and applies an AI tool with some pedagogical rationale and references one strength or limitation. | Identifies at least one AI tool and describes a basic use case with limited pedagogical reasoning. |
| **Learning Design Quality** | Produces a well-structured, innovative learning activity or resource that clearly promotes critical thinking and is directly applicable to their subject area. | Produces a relevant learning activity or resource with evidence of intentional design choices and some reference to learner needs. | Produces a basic learning activity or resource using AI with limited evidence of intentional design. |
| **Ethical Awareness** | Critically analyses at least two ethical considerations (e.g. bias, transparency, academic integrity) and proposes concrete classroom responses. | Identifies two or more ethical considerations and reflects on how these might affect teaching practice. | Acknowledges at least one ethical consideration relevant to AI use in the classroom. |
| **Reflection Depth** | Submission demonstrates deep, critical reflection linking AI use to evidenced pedagogical impact and future professional development intentions. | Submission shows considered reflection with reference to pedagogical benefit and notes at least one area for development. | Submission includes a basic reflection that describes what was done with limited pedagogical analysis. |

